WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two explorers read the same pair of numbers and walk to
different places.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.960 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One travels across first; the other goes upward first.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.640 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How could a map rule stop the disagreement?

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.854 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will build a shared coordinate system so a short
pair of numbers names one exact point.

00:00:16.854 --> 00:00:17.334 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to locate and describe points on a
first-quadrant grid.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:28.974 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:30.334 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:35.743 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:35.743 --> 00:00:40.223 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to locate and describe points on a
first-quadrant grid.

00:00:40.223 --> 00:00:43.023 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:50.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:50.445 --> 00:00:53.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:55.605 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can find the origin and axes.

00:00:55.605 --> 00:00:57.885 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read horizontal first.

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:00.165 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can plot points at intersections.

00:01:00.165 --> 00:01:04.045 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:12.762 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:12.762 --> 00:01:17.642 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw horizontal and vertical lines at equal intervals on
squared paper.

00:01:17.642 --> 00:01:19.482 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a ruler if it helps.

00:01:19.482 --> 00:01:21.722 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The lines meet at intersections.

00:01:21.722 --> 00:01:27.962 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark a starting point in the lower left, then number the
horizontal and vertical directions from zero.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:31.461 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both sets of numbers need a clear, consistent scale.

00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:35.261 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point where the two numbered axes meet is the origin.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.581 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its coordinates are zero, zero.

00:01:36.581 --> 00:01:39.901 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Zero means no movement from the starting point in that
direction.

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:48.048 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The origin is a real point on the grid, not an empty space
between the first two labelled lines.

00:01:48.048 --> 00:01:53.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An ordered pair gives the horizontal coordinate first and
the vertical coordinate second.

00:01:53.328 --> 00:01:53.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We write the pair in brackets with a comma between the
numbers.

00:01:53.888 --> 00:02:01.488 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The order matters because each position in the pair has a
different job.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:05.716 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today every point stays on this first-quadrant map or its
boundary.

00:02:05.716 --> 00:02:08.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our first landmark has coordinates two, three.

00:02:08.755 --> 00:02:10.315 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start at the origin.

00:02:10.315 --> 00:02:14.596 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which direction should you travel for the two, and which
direction for the three?

00:02:14.596 --> 00:02:17.556 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine tracing the journey with your finger.

00:02:17.556 --> 00:02:22.274 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the destination in mind while we apply the
horizontal-first convention.

00:02:27.274 --> 00:02:31.034 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Travel two units right from the origin, then three units
upward.

00:02:31.034 --> 00:02:31.314 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark the intersection reached by those movements.

00:02:31.314 --> 00:02:34.074 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point is two, three.

00:02:34.074 --> 00:02:42.339 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The path helps us locate it, but the ordered pair names the
destination, not every step of the path used to arrive
there.

00:02:42.339 --> 00:02:46.899 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To read the same point, look straight down to find its
horizontal coordinate.

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:50.379 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then look straight across to find its vertical coordinate.

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:52.739 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record those numbers in that order.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:58.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can check our reading by plotting the pair again and
seeing whether it returns to the original intersection.

00:02:58.871 --> 00:03:02.511 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A coordinate point sits at the crossing of two grid lines.

00:03:02.511 --> 00:03:05.591 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not refer to the centre of a square cell.

00:03:05.591 --> 00:03:10.511 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count intervals between grid lines rather than counting
painted squares at random.

00:03:10.511 --> 00:03:15.424 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A clear dot at the intersection removes doubt about the
location being described.

00:03:15.424 --> 00:03:17.824 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now compare two, three with three, two.

00:03:17.824 --> 00:03:20.584 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first point is two across and three up.

00:03:20.584 --> 00:03:23.384 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second is three across and two up.

00:03:23.384 --> 00:03:26.944 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both numbers appear in both pairs, yet the points differ.

00:03:26.944 --> 00:03:32.712 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Swapping numbers usually changes a location because it
changes the job of each number.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:37.712 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A flag is five units across and one unit above the
horizontal axis.

00:03:37.712 --> 00:03:41.112 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict its ordered pair before the label appears.

00:03:41.112 --> 00:03:44.072 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask yourself which number should be written first.

00:03:44.072 --> 00:03:48.564 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can check your choice by describing the journey from the
origin aloud.

00:03:53.564 --> 00:03:54.684 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The flag is at five, one.

00:03:54.684 --> 00:04:01.004 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five gives the horizontal position and one gives the
vertical position.

00:04:01.004 --> 00:04:01.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To check, return to the origin, move five right and one up.

00:04:01.164 --> 00:04:07.974 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The check returns us to the flag, so the words, numbers and
drawing agree.

00:04:07.974 --> 00:04:12.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point at zero, four needs no horizontal movement and four
units upward.

00:04:12.574 --> 00:04:14.894 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It lies on the vertical axis.

00:04:14.894 --> 00:04:19.134 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point at four, zero lies on the horizontal axis instead.

00:04:19.134 --> 00:04:24.751 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A zero coordinate is useful information, so do not omit it
when writing either pair.

00:04:24.751 --> 00:04:25.431 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each gap on our grid represents one unit.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:31.831 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On another map, a gap could represent two units or ten
metres.

00:04:31.831 --> 00:04:35.031 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read the labels before interpreting a position.

00:04:35.031 --> 00:04:42.143 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Counting three gaps does not automatically mean the
coordinate is three unless the stated scale makes each gap
one unit.

00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:46.583 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A helpful instruction gives a named point and its complete
ordered pair.

00:04:46.583 --> 00:04:51.063 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Saying that the treasure is near the top is too vague for
another explorer.

00:04:51.063 --> 00:04:56.354 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Saying that it is at two, three lets someone place the same
point without seeing your original map.

00:04:56.354 --> 00:05:02.394 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student labels a point four, zero but draws it on the
vertical axis above the origin.

00:05:02.394 --> 00:05:04.114 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide what went wrong.

00:05:04.114 --> 00:05:08.954 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which direction does the first number describe, and what
does the zero require?

00:05:08.954 --> 00:05:12.637 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain the correction rather than merely replacing the dot.

00:05:17.637 --> 00:05:22.437 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point four, zero belongs four units right along the
horizontal axis.

00:05:22.437 --> 00:05:25.797 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its second coordinate requires no upward movement.

00:05:25.797 --> 00:05:29.837 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mistake was exchanging the jobs of the two coordinates.

00:05:29.837 --> 00:05:34.663 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always check both the order and the scale, especially when a
point lies on an axis.

00:05:34.663 --> 00:05:36.503 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:36.503 --> 00:05:39.903 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Locate and describe points on a first-quadrant grid.

00:05:39.903 --> 00:05:44.903 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:44.903 --> 00:05:49.543 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:49.543 --> 00:05:56.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
